    "display_title": "Grounding and Essence as Generalized Identity",
    "authors": [
      "Fabrice Correia",
      "Alexander Skiles"
    ]
  },
  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "Grounding and essence can both be analyzed in terms of generalized identity, yielding a unified and uniform account of how the two metaphysical notions interact.",
      "short_title": "Generalized identity unifies metaphysics",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "We develop a unified and uniform account of grounding and essence, one which understands them both in terms of a generalized notion of identity examined in recent work by Fabrice Correia, Cian Dorr, Agustín Rayo, and others."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Generalized identity is a non-factive no-difference operator that can apply to factual and generic contents without requiring fact-shaped or property-shaped entities as its relata.",
      "short_title": "Generalized identity is nonfactive",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "we express generalized identity with an operator, º, indexed by zero or more variables, which takes two open or closed sentences and yields another. Where p and q are open or closed sentences, (5) pºq should be read as 'For it to be the case that p is for it to be the case that q'.",
        "we read such statements as not requiring that the flanking expressions be true/satisfied: for instance, it is intuitively no objection to (1) that there aren’t any bachelors. For short, we read them non-factively."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Full generic essence is captured by generalized identity: being F is what it is to be G in full exactly when, for a thing to be G, is for it to be F.",
      "short_title": "Full essence is identity",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "FULL-GENERIC-ESSENCE Being F is what it is to be G in full iff: for a thing to be G is for it to be F (in symbols: Gx ºx Fx)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Partial generic essence is captured by conjunctive partial generalized identity: being F is partially what it is to be G exactly when being G is being both F and some H.",
      "short_title": "Partial essence is conjunctive",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "PARTIAL-GENERIC-ESSENCE Being F is partially what it is to be G iff: there is some H such that for a thing to be G is for it to be both F and H (in symbols: Gx ºx Fx Ù Hx)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Objectual essence can be accounted for through generic essence, so a feature is what an object is in full or in part according to whether it is what being identical to that object is in full or in part.",
      "short_title": "Objectual essence tracks identity",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "Following Correia (2006, pp. 764-5) we account for objectual essence in terms of generic essence: FULL-OBJECTUAL-ESSENCE Being F is what x is in full iff: being F is what it is to be identical to x in full."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Factual grounding is analyzed through disjunctive parthood: a plurality of facts grounds q exactly when their conjunction is a disjunctive part of q and q is not a relevant conjunctive-disjunctive part of any ground.",
      "short_title": "Grounding uses disjunctive parthood",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "FACTUAL-GROUNDING p1, p2, … < q iff: (i) p1 Ù p2 Ù … ⊑∨ q; and (ii) neither q ⊑∧∨ p1, nor q ⊑∧∨ p2, nor …"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "A subsumption-theoretic version of grounding says that p1, p2, and so on ground q exactly when their conjunction is subsumed by q while q is not quasi-subsumed by any of them.",
      "short_title": "Subsumption refines grounding",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "Say that p is quasi-subsumed by q (in symbols: p ≲ q) iff there is some r such that p ∧ r ≤ q. Replacing ⊑∨ with ≤ in our original account of grounding, and ⊑∧∨ with ≲, delivers the following subsumption-theoretic analogue: (18) p1, p2, … < q iff: (i) p1 Ù p 2 Ù … ≤ q; and (ii) neither q ≲ p1, nor q ≲ p2, nor …"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Ground-theoretic status is preserved under factual identity because the proposed account combines generalized identity with symmetric and transitive identity principles.",
      "short_title": "Identity preserves grounding status",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "given that generalized identity is symmetric and transitive, and that the rules R3 and R4 are truth-preserving, FACTUAL-GROUNDING entails that ground-theoretic status is preserved under factual identity. More precisely: (17) a. If p, p1, p2 … ground q and p º p* , then p*, p1, p2, … ground q. b. If p1, p2, … ground q and q º q*, then p1, p2, … ground q*."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "If being F grounds being G, then the fact that being F necessitates being G can be derived from what it is to be G.",
      "short_title": "Grounding yields necessitation",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "if being F grounds being G, our account implies that being G just is being F or H for some given H, and which on our account gives the (or a) full essence of being G. It then follows that necessarily, a thing is G iff it is F or H, which entails by straightforward modal reasoning that necessarily, a thing is F only if it’s also G, as desired."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Necessary entailment or equivalence is not sufficient for essence, because generalized identity and grounding can distinguish essential structure from merely necessary coextension.",
      "short_title": "Necessity underdetermines essence",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "being Socrates and such that there are infinitely many prime numbers is necessarily entailed by and equivalent to being Socrates, yet presumably the former is ground-theoretically linked to the arithmetic realm in ways that the latter isn’t. Given our account, this provides reason to believe that the former isn’t part of the essence of the latter either, as desired."
      ]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_items": [
    {
      "evidence_id": "evidence-abstract-unification-thesis--e857615fb05e",
      "title": "Unified Identity Account",
      "excerpt": "We develop a unified and uniform account of grounding and essence, one which understands them both in terms of a generalized notion of identity examined in recent work by Fabrice Correia, Cian Dorr, Agustín Rayo, and others. We argue that the account comports with antecedently plausible principles governing grounding, essence, and identity taken individually, and illuminates how the three interact.",
      "overview": "The abstract states the paper’s central formal ambition: grounding and essence are not treated as independent primitive notions, but as two applications of generalized identity. This evidence grounds the overall unification claim while leaving detailed adequacy arguments to later definitions and comparisons.",
      "evidence_type": "formal_argument",
      "claim_refs": [
        "0"
      ],
      "source_id": "source-03-grounding-essence-and-identity--e857615fb05e",
      "page_refs": [
        "p.1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "evidence_id": "evidence-nonfactive-no-difference--a1b2c3d4e5f6",
      "title": "No-Difference Operator",
      "excerpt": "Although it’s tempting to read 'For … is for ___' statements as indicating a type of priority of one side to the other, º is instead to be read as what Rayo evocatively calls a 'no-difference operator'. Accordingly, we take it to behave like the familiar no-difference predicate for what we’ve called objectual identity (a.k.a. =). As such, we take º to be reflexive, symmetric, and transitive.",
      "overview": "This passage fixes the logical role of generalized identity. The operator is not itself an explanatory-priority relation; it behaves like identity, which is why it can underwrite later accounts of both essence and grounding without reducing either to ordinary objectual identity.",
      "evidence_type": "formal_argument",
      "claim_refs": [
        "1"
      ],
      "source_id": "source-03-grounding-essence-and-identity--e857615fb05e",
      "page_refs": [
        "pp.4-5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "evidence_id": "evidence-full-generic-essence--b7c8d9e0f1a2",
      "title": "Full Essence Formula",
      "excerpt": "FULL-GENERIC-ESSENCE Being F is what it is to be G in full iff: for a thing to be G is for it to be F (in symbols: Gx ºx Fx).",
      "overview": "This definition is a compact formal artifact for the essence side of the account. It identifies full generic essence with generalized identity, making essence a matter of what it is for something to satisfy a predicate rather than a merely modal necessity claim.",
      "evidence_type": "equation",
      "claim_refs": [
